Counts: V7.verdicts.supports.studies = 20, V7.verdicts.limits_scope.studies = 7, V7.verdicts.challenges.studies = 0. V7.tested_sharing_an_author_with_an_original = 0, and V7.original_sources_linked is empty, so all tests come from groups other than the original's. These counts were built before the checks. The checks would make arXiv:2604.27019's V7 verdicts limits_scope only, and the check on V7.support[5] would do the same for gh:kemalderya/refusal-direction-dpo. They would also turn arXiv:2608.25390's limits_scope verdict into supports, leaving only its layer-7 overlap result (geom_overlap7) as a possible limits_scope. The counts are not recomputed here.

Several independent groups show that training choices change whether a single direction can be removed:
- training under ablation: ReSAT (https://openreview.net/forum?id=jDhBl5eznH) and Fail-closed (arXiv:2602.16977);
- training against direction separability, not under ablation: INSEP (https://openreview.net/forum?id=Brsc62CHyu), a LoRA adapter trained to reduce DIM-direction separability with behaviour-preserving losses, tested on eight bases from six families;
- response form: 2505.19056 extended refusals. 2608.25390 runs controlled SFT of OLMo-2-0425-1B-Instruct with 1 to 16 distinct refusal starts, at checkpoints matched to refusal rate 0.6 (a fraction). More starts give higher sr(ΔH) and smaller ablation deltas, so the degree changes but the direction still exists;
- defence training: 2503.11185 DeepAlign (RFA rows r02, r05, r13, r16, r19, r21, r23) combines a contrastive hidden-state loss with diverse non-refusal responses. The authors credit the diverse responses, but the RFA results do not separate the two parts, and the authors' isolating ablation (Table VIII) covers only SCAV, DrAttack and CodeAttack;
- stage, from released checkpoints only: 2504.02904; 2608.25390, with no attackable direction at base, a functional one by SFT and larger deltas later (cp_base, cp_sft, cp_inst).

Limits:
- Some trainings change the degree or location, not whether the direction exists. 2504.18872. 2604.27019: the carrier moves from layer 24 to layer 0, rank stays at 1.23 to 1.27, and ablation never removes refusal. gh:kemalderya/refusal-direction-dpo: DPO restoration re-forms one direction at L15 instead of L21. Also the 2608.25390 controlled SFT above.
- The same recipe gives different results on different models. In 2503.11185 r03, Circuit Breaker on Llama-3-8B-Instruct leaves RFA ASR at 66.0% (92.2% undefended). See also 2607.02714 r32.
- Much of the support comes from objectives built as defences: training under ablation and INSEP. Per the check on V7.wording_issues[3], extended-refusal fine-tuning (plain SFT, but designed to defeat abliteration) and DeepAlign (motivated against refusal-direction nullification) are also defences.

Caveat, not a limit: the stage evidence comes from released checkpoints that also differ in objectives, data, training histories and update counts. 2608.25390 calls its own checkpoint comparison descriptive (Section 4.2, App. D).
